WHAT THIS IS FOR, PLAINLY

This repository is public so that the app can be served from GitHub Pages and so that
anyone can see how it works. It is not published for reuse. No permission is granted to
copy, modify, redistribute, host, or make derivative works of this software, in whole or
in part, for any purpose.

Two exceptions, because they are what a public repository is for:

  1. Reading it. Look at it, learn from it, quote it in a discussion, point at it.
  2. The rights GitHub's own Terms of Service grant every user of a public repository —
     viewing and forking it within GitHub. Forking is not a licence to publish.

WHAT A LICENCE CANNOT DO, AND IT IS BETTER TO SAY SO

Element 26 is a web app. Every line of it is delivered to a browser in order to run, so
anybody who loads the page already has the source, and no licence, minifier or obfuscator
changes that. This file states terms; it does not enforce them, and it should not be
mistaken for a technical control.

The things that actually need protecting are not protected by this file either. They are
protected by design: the API keys live as Cloudflare Worker secrets and appear nowhere in
this repository, the Workers refuse any origin but the published site's, and a person's
training data is reachable only with both halves of their credential. A copy of this app
hosted somewhere else is a copy of the front end and nothing more — it cannot reach the
account service, it cannot read anybody's log, and it cannot spend anybody's API quota.

For any other use, ask.
